{
  "title": "Spree Commerce \u2014 eCommerce / Marketplace (15,427\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: Spree Commerce\nDomain: eCommerce / Marketplace | Scale: large | Stars: 15,427\nProblem: Spree is an open-source headless eCommerce platform enabling businesses to launch cross-border storefronts, B2B wholesale marketplaces, and multi-tenant SaaS solutions with a complete REST API and production-ready storefront.\nTarget users: B2B wholesale merchants, Multi-vendor marketplace operators, Cross-border eCommerce businesses, SaaS eCommerce providers, Enterprise retailers\nCore features: REST API with TypeScript SDK and OpenAPI 3.0 spec; Next.js storefront with multi-region routing and Stripe/Klarna/Affirm payments; Cross-border markets with currency, language, and payment localization; Advanced pricing and promotions engine with price lists and customer groups; Admin dashboard with role-based permissions and Tailwind CSS customization; MeiliSearch integration for typo-tolerant product search and faceted filtering; Event Bus and Webhooks 2.0 for extensibility and integrations; CSV product import/export and bulk operations\nIntegrations: Stripe, Adyen, PayPal, Google Analytics 4, Google Tag Manager, Klaviyo, MeiliSearch\nTech stack: Next.js 16, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS 4 + Ruby on Rails + PostgreSQL (inferred from Rails conventions) + Docker, Render, Turbo monorepo\nKey libraries: Spree gem, TypeScript SDK, Base UI React, Biome (linting)\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Headless eCommerce design separates API backend (Rails) from frontend consumption, enabling multi-storefront and custom client implementations\n- Turbo monorepo structure manages multiple interdependent packages (backend, SDK, storefront, admin) with unified tooling and shared development experience\n- Provider-agnostic payment sessions abstraction allows pluggable payment processors without storefront code changes, reducing coupling\n- Event Bus and Webhooks 2.0 enable ecosystem extensibility and third-party integrations while maintaining backward compatibility",
